WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
 - Hello and welcome back to Hardware Architecture.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.560
 Now you might ask, you know, why do I tell you

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.140
 about hardware architecture?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.400
 You're not, probably you're not going to build any hardware,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.840
 although it's fun stuff to do, and if you're going to become

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.320
 a computer scientist, which most of you won't want to be,

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.120
 it's a great thing to study, and it's a,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.360
 those who build our hardware are amazingly talented

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.560
 individuals, and it's a really rewarding job.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.560
 The reason I like talking to you about hardware

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.080
 is because I want to be able to use words

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.280
 at some point and say, oh, secondary storage,

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.120
 or central processing unit, or random access memory,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.520
 or peripherals, you know, input devices,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.760
 and I want to be able to say those words,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.560
 and I want you to be able to understand them.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.900
 And so I got, I'll start with a little piece of hardware

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.080
 called the Raspberry Pi, and the Raspberry Pi

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.160
 is a cute little single board computer.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.440
 As we go forward, these things are smaller

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.360
 and smaller and smaller, and the interesting thing is

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.880
 is that the architecture of these stays the same,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.400
 but the number of components drops.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.240
 So I'm going to start and give you a block diagram

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.920
 of sort of a generic computer,

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.280
 and tell you the major parts of it.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.840
 Now, I'm going to show you some really old hardware,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.640
 some really new hardware, and then some hardware

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.560
 that is of medium age, and the medium age hardware

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.360
 is probably the easiest one to see.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.840
 The architecture is the same, okay, and so,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.600
 the basic block diagram is that the brains,

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.920
 if there are brains in computers, which there really aren't,

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.560
 the software is the closest thing computers have to brains,

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.840
 but in hardware, the closest brain a computer has is this,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.040
 called a microprocessing unit, or a central processor unit,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.300
 and this is designed, you know, three billion times a sec,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.520
 three billion times a second, to ask the question,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.120
 what do you want me to do next?

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.440
 And these little pins on the back are our instructions,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.720
 like 32 or 64 of these pins, three billion times a second,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.020
 we send an instruction into these things.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.920
 Now, we can't sit there and talk to it, we can't,

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.840
 and so the instructions we store

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.880
 in what's called the main memory,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.760
 and this memory is really fast,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.280
 and the memory sort of feeds this,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.520
 and so every time the CPU needs a new instruction,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.040
 it asks the memory where that instruction is,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.280
 and so the memory feeds the instruction to the CPU,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.600
 the CPU does it, says, give me another instruction,

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.720
 CPU does it, gives me another instruction,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.600
 and that is the basic essence of programming.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.760
 This asks what's next, and this is where your program

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.040
 is stored, or a program you purchased

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.160
 or came with your hardware, where that's all stored,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.800
 and those are your places, and so you end up inside,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.120
 your programs end up inside this memory.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.880
 So then there's, I mean, and so in software,

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.040
 you tend to program the CPU, and if you had bought

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.600
 a desktop computer a number of years back,

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.760
 it would have this thing called the motherboard,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.640
 and the motherboard is called this because it kind of

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.680
 connects all the components together,

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.660
 and so if you buy memory by itself, it does nothing,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.240
 but it has a place to plug into the motherboard,

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.120
 and if you buy a microprocessor,

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.900
 it has a place to plug into the motherboard,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.320
 and if you buy a hard drive,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.200
 this is a really old hard drive,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.920
 it has a place to plug in on the motherboard,

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.680
 and so the motherboard sort of connects everything together.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.920
 The hard drive is secondary storage.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.080
 Now, the way, how secondary storage is different

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.200
 than the main memory, which, there it is,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.400
 I got to un-pile this stuff.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.240
 So this main memory is really fast,

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.440
 but as soon as you turn the power off of this memory,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.160
 it sort of vanishes, and so to store files

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.560
 like word processing files or text files or whatever,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:00.640
 you got to store it on something

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.360
 that lasts a little bit longer,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.800
 and so that's the purpose of the secondary storage.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.000
 It's permanent.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.080
 When the power's off, it stores it.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.520
 Now, this one here is in such bad shape

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.620
 that it isn't probably storing anything,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.400
 but it's got these little heads,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.560
 and it spins around and goes in and out,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.200
 and we'll have a video later

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.280
 that shows you one of these things

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.760
 that's not quite in as bad a shape.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.600
 If you look, this has four different platters

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.400
 that are all spinning around,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.320
 and so this is just using magnetic material

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.120
 and electronics that sort of magnetize

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.760
 and demagnetize this stuff,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.640
 and if you look at a disc, they're often rated,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.280
 physical discs are rated in revolutions per minute,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.360
 and that's how many times this thing spins around,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.440
 and if you got an old desktop and you hear it spin up,

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.360
 this is the thing that's spinning,

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.160
 and it's the place that your operating system lives,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.800
 your files live, your applications live

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.200
 while they're stored and while the computer's turned off,

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.740
 and then they're loaded into this while they're running,

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.580
 and then

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:11.640
 this CPU takes the data from the main memory

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:16.560
 and your program runs at three billion operations per second.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.200
 So let's talk a little bit about something

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:25.280
 that this is probably from the 1960s or 70s.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.960
 This actually has, if you're an electrical person,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.960
 it has capacitors, those little silver things are capacitors,

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.260
 these little colored things are resistors,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.400
 and that's more capacitors, and then there's wires,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.120
 and wires move everything,

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.760
 and so when you say this has millions of transistors,

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.640
 oh wait, that isn't a capacitor, that's a transistor,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.800
 that's a transistor, when you say that this here has etched,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.000
 and if you look closely at this,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.880
 go look at a picture of a microprocessor online,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.960
 you will see that it has millions of these,

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.960
 and so the difference between 1960 and today

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.960
 is this circuitry of capacitors, resistors,

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.960
 and transistors has been microized and put onto this.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.120
 It's using a photographic process,

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.400
 and they're tinier and tinier and putting more and more on,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.640
 and if you think going from millions of these

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.480
 to one of these is crazy, the thing that's happening now,

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.400
 and the reason we have whole computers inside our pocket,

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:35.480
 is that everything, all of this, this whole thing,

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:39.340
 CPU, memory, everything, all of it connected,

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.600
 and the storage is being made smaller and smaller,

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.920
 and so this little single board computer, called a Raspberry Pi,

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.960
 has one thing in it, and it has the main memory,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.000
 and it has the CPU, it has connections for things

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.000
 like peripherals, like keyboards and stuff,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.720
 now it doesn't yet have secondary storage on it,

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.000
 the secondary storage gets plugged in right here via USB,

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.600
 and then if you take it one step farther to my phone,

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.840
 it's got the secondary storage built right in,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:11.740
 and so this picture goes from the size of cabinets

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.560
 in the old days, all the way down to really tiny,

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.160
 but at the end of the day, inside it is a highly

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:22.720
 sophisticated piece of circuitry that asks for instructions

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.960
 one at a time, and main memory that holds the instructions

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.960
 and feeds them, okay?

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.840
 Central processor does the thinking,

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.660
 let's take a look here, central processor does the thinking,

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:36.400
 it runs the program, it's asking what's next,

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.400
 it's not really smart, but it's really fast,

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:43.360
 and so we compensate for the lack of intelligence

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:46.320
 of this thing by us writing really good software

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.280
 that runs really fast, and so voice recognition

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.640
 on things like phones is possible because computers

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.060
 have so much storage and they run so fast,

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:57.980
 and the algorithms that do voice recognition

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.280
 are finally starting to work.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.440
 Input devices like keyboards and mice and pens and whatever,

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.260
 they come in, output devices are like the screens

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.660
 that we see, the main memory is the fast part

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.760
 of the computer that stores all the programs,

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:16.040
 and the secondary memory is the permanent storage.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:21.040
 Increasingly, secondary memory, do I have any USB sticks

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.840
 in here, I don't, well, increasingly secondary memory

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.840
 is flash RAM or static RAM with no moving parts,

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:36.840
 and so in a few years you'll not even be able to see

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:40.680
 secondary memory with moving parts, but that's okay,

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:43.880
 it's still secondary memory, it's still memory that lasts,

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:48.440
 and so you and where your place is in here

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.000
 is you live in the main memory, this is you, you are here,

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.120
 and so in a sense when the CPU asks the question what next,

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.560
 it is your job to answer that, and you answer that

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:01.920
 by writing Python code, and so your Python code,

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.240
 you'll write a file in Python code, blah, blah, blah,

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.280
 blah, and then that Python code sort of gets loaded

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.560
 into main memory, there's a magic translation process

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.120
 that happens, and then your code is actually answering

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.360
 this question three billion times a second,

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:18.800
 three billion times a second, you're sitting there,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.880
 but this is you, you're really out here,

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.160
 but you then write a file, and the file's loaded in,

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.520
 and then the file runs, and that's how things are at,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.340
 and that's your place in the world.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:33.460
 Now, what's actually running is not Python code,

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.120
 there is, as I said, a translation process,

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.280
 you write a Python file, and then Python itself

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:44.440
 translates this into the actual language known by

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.320
 the microprocessor, which is a series of zeros and ones

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.560
 called machine language, someday I would love to teach you

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.760
 a class on machine language, but for now we're going to teach

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.240
 you Python, and we're going to use Python as a crutch,

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:57.940
 we don't have to talk machine language, but you could,

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:00.420
 if you really wanted to, you could know how to write

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:04.000
 machine language, but I assure you, Python is far easier

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.440
 to learn than machine language, so Python acts

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.480
 as a translator, translates what you're doing

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.680
 into machine language, and then the machine language

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.440
 is what's sent back and forth, but still,

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.440
 even though it's translated to machine language,

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.200
 it's you, it is you answering those questions,

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.120
 and that's what a program is, is you pre-storing

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:26.960
 your response to the what next question over and over again.

